## Why This Is Asked

Understanding three-way partitioning shows depth beyond basic quicksort, especially for duplicates. It also tests in-place mutation and careful loop invariants.

## Key Concepts

- Three-way partitioning around a pivot
- In-place sorting with O(1) extra space
- Average vs worst-case time, handling duplicates

## Code Example

```javascript
function threeWayQuicksort(arr, left=0, right=arr.length-1) {
  if (left >= right) return;
  const pivot = arr[Math.floor((left + right) / 2)];
  let lt = left, i = left, gt = right;
  while (i <= gt) {
    if (arr[i] < pivot) { [arr[lt], arr[i]] = [arr[i], arr[lt]]; lt++; i++; }
    else if (arr[i] > pivot) { [arr[i], arr[gt]] = [arr[gt], arr[i]]; gt--; }
    else { i++; }
  }
  threeWayQuicksort(arr, left, lt - 1);
  threeWayQuicksort(arr, gt + 1, right);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you mitigate worst-case performance in practice?
- How would you adapt the algorithm to a custom comparator or stable variants?

