## Why This Is Asked

Standard quicksort performs well on average but can degrade to O(n²) on adversarial inputs, particularly with many duplicates. Introsort-style hybrids combine the practical efficiency of quicksort with worst-case guarantees, making-world engineering this a common real constraint.

## Key Concepts

- **3-way partitioning (Dutch National Flag)**: Partitions array into elements less than, equal to, and greater than the pivot, dramatically reducing comparisons for datasets with many duplicates
- **Recursion depth tracking**: Prevents stack overflow and performance degradation on pathological inputs
- **Hybrid switching**: Falls back to mergesort or heapsort when depth exceeds 2*log2(N) or partition size drops below a threshold (~16 elements)
- **Worst-case guarantees**: Ensures O(n log n) time complexity regardless of input distribution

## Code Example

```javascript
function hybridSort(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  const maxDepth = 2 * Math.floor(Math.log2(n));

  function quickSort3Way(lo, hi, depth) {
    if (hi - lo < 16) {
      insertionSort(arr, lo, hi);
      return;
    }
    if (depth > maxDepth) {
      mergeSort(arr, lo, hi);
      return;
    }

    const [lt, gt] = partition3Way(arr, lo, hi);
    quickSort3Way(lo, lt - 1, depth + 1);
    quickSort3Way(gt + 1, hi, depth + 1);
  }

  quickSort3Way(0, n - 1, 0);
  return arr;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to combine hash tables with heaps for real-time top-K queries under sliding-window expirations. It evaluates understanding of lazy cleanup techniques, time-based eviction strategies, and maintaining data consistency under concurrent updates.

## Key Concepts
- Hash map for O(1) task lookups and metadata storage
- Max-heap for efficient top-K ordering
- Lazy deletion to avoid expensive per-update heap removals
- Time-based expiration using timestamp validation

## Code Example
```javascript
class Leaderboard {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.map = new Map(); // task_id -> {score, timestamp}
    this.heap = new MaxHeap(); // (score, task_id)
  }

  update(task_id, delta, ts) {
    const current = this.map.get(task_id) || { score: 0, timestamp: 0 };
    const updated = { 
      score: current.score + delta, 
      timestamp: ts 
    };
    this.map.set(task_id, updated);
    this.heap.push([updated.score, task_id]);
  }

  getTopK(k, current_ts) {
    const result = [];
    const temp = [];

    while (result.length < k && !this.heap.isEmpty()) {
      const [score, task_id] = this.heap.pop();
      const task = this.map.get(task_id);

      if (task && task.score === score && current_ts - task.timestamp <= this.W) {
        result.push({ task_id, score });
      } else {
        temp.push([score, task_id]);
      }
    }

    // Restore invalid entries
    temp.forEach(entry => this.heap.push(entry));

    return result;
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- **Update**: O(1) hash map + O(log n) heap push = O(log n)
- **Top-K query**: O(k log n) worst case due to lazy deletion
- **Space**: O(n) for hash map + heap (potentially O(n + m) where m are stale entries)
- **Expiration**: Handled during queries via timestamp validation

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of two classic sorts, practical optimization, and memory trade-offs. A hybrid approach reveals ability to tune algorithms for real data patterns.

## Key Concepts
- Hybrid sorting threshold and when it helps
- Stability via merge vs insertion behavior
- Space usage with a single auxiliary buffer

## Code Example
```javascript
function sortBatch(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  const buf = new Array(n);
  function insertionSort(a, left, right) {
    for (let i = left + 1; i <= right; i++) {
      const key = a[i];
      let j = i - 1;
      while (j >= left && a[j] > key) {
        a[j + 1] = a[j];
        j--;
      }
      a[j + 1] = key;
    }
  }
  function merge(left, mid, right) {
    let i = left, j = mid + 1, k = left;
    while (i <= mid && j <= right) {
      if (arr[i] <= arr[j]) buf[k++] = arr[i++];
      else buf[k++] = arr[j++];
    }
    while (i <= mid) buf[k++] = arr[i++];
    while (j <= right) buf[k++] = arr[j++];
    for (let t = left; t <= right; t++) arr[t] = buf[t];
  }
  function sort(l, r) {
    if (l >= r) return;
    if (r - l + 1 <= 16) {
      insertionSort(arr, l, r);
    } else {
      const m = Math.floor((l + r) / 2);
      sort(l, m);
      sort(m + 1, r);
      merge(l, m, r);
    }
  }
  sort(0, n - 1);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine weighted shortest paths with path counting via a DAG, a common real-world need in routing and logistics.

## Key Concepts
- Dijkstra for non-negative weights
- Shortest-path DAG construction
- Topological DP to count paths
- Time/space trade-offs and modular arithmetic

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode outline for counting shortest paths
function countShortestPaths(graph, s, t) {
  const dist = dijkstra(graph, s);
  const dag = buildShortestPathDag(graph, dist);
  const order = topologicalSort(dag);
  const ways = new Array(graph.n).fill(0);
  ways[s] = 1;
  for (const u of order) {
    for (const [v] of dag.adj[u]) {
      ways[v] = (ways[v] + ways[u]) % 1000000007;
    }
  }
  return { dist: dist[t], count: ways[t] };
}
```

